   "response_text": "Yo! All four dug straight outta the source. \ud83d\udc36\n\n**1) Minimum Bash version** \u2014 `src/click/shell_completion.py:92` literally says `# Only Bash >= 4.4 has the nosort option.`, and the generated setup uses `complete -o nosort`. So: **4.4**.\n\n**2) Classes inheriting from ClickException** \u2014 walking `src/click/exceptions.py`:\n- `UsageError(ClickException)` \u2192 direct\n- `BadParameter(UsageError)` \u2192 indirect\n- `MissingParameter(BadParameter)` \u2192 indirect\n- `NoSuchOption(UsageError)` \u2192 indirect\n- `BadOptionUsage(UsageError)` \u2192 indirect\n- `BadArgumentUsage(UsageError)` \u2192 indirect\n- `FileError(ClickException)` \u2192 direct\n\nThat's **7** (excluding `ClickException`). `Abort` and `Exit` inherit from `RuntimeError`, so they don't count.\n\n**3) Env var for `my-tool`** \u2014 `core.py:1143` builds it as `_{prog_name with '-'\u2192'_', '.'\u2192'_'}_COMPLETE`.upper(). So `my-tool` \u2192 **`_MY_TOOL_COMPLETE`**.\n\n**4) Direct base class of `Exit`** \u2014 `class Exit(RuntimeError)` \u2192 **RuntimeError**.\n\nSanity check on the env var logic: prog_name.replace(\"-\",\"_\").replace(\".\",\"_\") on \"my-tool\" gives \"my_tool\", wrapped as \"_my_tool_COMPLETE\", uppercased \u2192 `_MY_TOOL_COMPLETE`. \u2705\n\nANSWER: 4.4|7|_MY_TOOL_COMPLETE|RuntimeError",
